{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K3LZodrPLm5mKf5Dh5BNdg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/kubernetes-cloud-native-associate/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"The Linux Foundation / CNCF\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"exam-format\", \"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-exam-facts\", \"domain-weights\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) — Linux Foundation exam page\n9\t\n10\t\"The Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) exam demonstrates a user's foundational knowledge and skills in Kubernetes and the wider cloud native ecosystem.\" Experience level: Beginner. Prerequisites: none.\n11\t\n12\t## Exam details\n13\t- Format: online, proctored, multiple-choice exam\n14\t- Duration: 90 minutes\n15\t- Pricing: exam only $250; exam + THRIVE-ONE annual subscription $495; exam + LFS250 course $299\n16\t- Included: 12-month eligibility window to schedule and take the exam; two exam attempts; certification valid for 2 years; exam preparation handbook\n17\t\n18\t## Domains & competencies\n19\t- Kubernetes Fundamentals — 44% (Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization)\n20\t- Container Orchestration — 28% (Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage)\n21\t- Cloud Native Application Delivery — 16% (Application Delivery; Debugging)\n22\t- Cloud Native Architecture — 12% (Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration)\n23\t\n24\t## Not stated on this page\n25\tQuestion count and passing score are not published on the exam page. Third-party sources widely report 60 questions and a 75% passing score; treat those as commonly reported, not official.\n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e31eebcc-5cdf-41f6-9a4d-ad3448ff2836","uuid":"52cd8339-5b18-41d5-9611-cb4e5ba24285","timestamp":"2026-08-24T03:42:18.964Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\sources\\lf-kcna-exam-page-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://training.linuxfoundation.org/certification/kubernetes-cloud-native-associate/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"The Linux Foundation / CNCF\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"exam-format\", \"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-exam-facts\", \"domain-weights\"]\n---\n# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) — Linux Foundation exam page\n\n\"The Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) exam demonstrates a user's foundational knowledge and skills in Kubernetes and the wider cloud native ecosystem.\" Experience level: Beginner. Prerequisites: none.\n\n## Exam details\n- Format: online, proctored, multiple-choice exam\n- Duration: 90 minutes\n- Pricing: exam only $250; exam + THRIVE-ONE annual subscription $495; exam + LFS250 course $299\n- Included: 12-month eligibility window to schedule and take the exam; two exam attempts; certification valid for 2 years; exam preparation handbook\n\n## Domains & competencies\n- Kubernetes Fundamentals — 44% (Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization)\n- Container Orchestration — 28% (Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage)\n- Cloud Native Application Delivery — 16% (Application Delivery; Debugging)\n- Cloud Native Architecture — 12% (Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration)\n\n## Not stated on this page\nQuestion count and passing score are not published on the exam page. Third-party sources widely report 60 questions and a 75% passing score; treat those as commonly reported, not official.\n","numLines":26,"startLine":1,"totalLines":26}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AQ33ffPGzQDSpDFw1xXhKT","type":"tool_result","content":"19:| **I — Taking Departure** | What KCNA is, what changed, and how to read this book | 1 | — (orientation) | 0% |\n20-| **II — Ship, Cargo, and Company** | Containers, the cluster, the objects that describe it, and the components that run it | 2–8 | D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals | **44%** |\n21-| **III — Underway** | Keeping workloads reachable, stored, secured, and diagnosable | 9–13 | D2 Container Orchestration | **28%** |\n22-| **IV — Dispatches** | Getting software from a repository into a cluster, and finding out why it didn't work | 14–16 | D3 Cloud Native Application Delivery | **16%** |\n23-| **V — The Wider Sea** | The patterns, the foundation, and the instruments | 17–18 | D4 Cloud Native Architecture | **12%** |\n24-| **VI — Making Port** | Synthesis, exam-day strategy, and the full mock | 19–20 | — (synthesis / assessment) | 0% |\n25-\n26-Content-chapter distribution: D1 **7 of 17 (41.2%)** · D2 **5 (29.4%)** · D3 **3 (17.6%)** · D4 **2 (11.8%)**. Target: 44 / 28 / 16 / 12. Maximum count deviation: 2.8 points.\n27-\n28----\n29-\n30-## Chapters\n31-\n32-| # | Title (working) | Subtitle (working) | Part | Covers objectives | Prerequisites | Exam weight % |\n33-|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n34:| 1 | Taking Departure | *\"Ninety minutes, four domains, and a curriculum that moved under everyone's feet\"* | I | — (exam mechanics; cloud native framing planted for Ch 17) | — | 0 |\n35-| 2 | Cargo in Standard Crates | *\"Why the shipping container beat the ship\"* | II | **D1.4** — containers vs VMs, images, layers, tags vs digests, registries, `imagePullPolicy`, immutability, OCI runtime/image/distribution specs, runC, CRI, containerd, CRI-O, RuntimeClass, build practices | — | 9 |\n36-| 3 | The Ship's Company | *\"Everyone aboard has one job, and none of them is 'be in charge'\"* | II | **D1.1** — deployment eras, what Kubernetes is and is not, control-plane components, node components, addons, controllers, the control loop, desired vs current state, Kubernetes origin and history | 2 | 6 |\n37-| 4 | Records of Intent | *\"You don't give Kubernetes orders. You file a declaration.\"* | II | **D1.1** — objects, spec/status, manifests, `apiVersion`/`kind`/`metadata`/`spec`, `kubectl apply`, labels, selectors (equality and set-based), annotations, namespaces, initial namespaces, namespaced vs cluster-scoped, ConfigMaps, Secrets (definition and contrast) | 3 | 6 |\n38-| 5 | The Smallest Vessel | *\"A Pod is not a container, and that distinction is worth points\"* | II | **D1.1** — Pod concept and network namespace, multi-container and init containers, Pod phases, container states, `restartPolicy`, restart backoff, liveness/readiness/startup probes, probe mechanisms, resource requests and limits, QoS classes, ServiceAccount as Pod identity (planted) | 2, 4 | 7 |\n39-| 6 | Fleets, Not Vessels | *\"Nobody sails one Pod\"* | II | **D1.1** — ReplicaSet, Deployment, rolling update, `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`, Recreate, revisions, rollout history, rollback, pause/resume, StatefulSet (introduced with siblings), DaemonSet, Job, CronJob, `kubectl scale`, HPA concept, custom resources and the operator pattern (definition) | 4, 5 | 6 |\n40-| 7 | Assigning the Berth | *\"Filter, score, bind — and then a coin flip\"* | II | **D1.3** — scheduling overview, feasible nodes, filtering, scoring, binding, random tie-break, unschedulable Pods, node labels, `nodeSelector`, node affinity (required vs preferred), pod affinity/anti-affinity, taints and tolerations (NoSchedule / PreferNoSchedule / NoExecute), `nodeName`, topology spread, scheduling policies vs profiles | 5, 6 | 5 |\n41-[Omitted long context line]\n42-| 9 | Every Pod Has an Address | *\"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that makes churn survivable\"* | III | **D2.1** — the Kubernetes network model, Pod IP and shared namespace, CNI, Service, ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, ExternalName, headless Services, Services without selectors, EndpointSlice, service proxy, kube-proxy modes, CoreDNS, Service and Pod DNS records, FQDN | 4, 5, 6 | 7 |\n43-[Omitted long context line]\n44-| 11 | Below the Waterline | *\"Storage outlives the Pod that asked for it\"* | III | **D2.4** — volume types (emptyDir, hostPath, configMap/secret, projected, ephemeral), PersistentVolume, PersistentVolumeClaim, StorageClass, static vs dynamic provisioning, binding, reclaim policies (Retain/Delete/Recycle), access modes (RWO/ROX/RWX/RWOP), CSI, StatefulSet + PV pairing (loop closed from Ch 6) | 4, 5, 6 | 5 |\n45-[Omitted long context line]\n46-| 13 | When the Cluster Won't Answer | *\"Read the phase before you read the logs\"* | III | **D2.3** — the two-audience split, Pod failure signatures (Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, ImagePullBackOff, ErrImagePull, OOMKilled, Evicted, CreateContainerConfigError), events, `kubectl describe`/`events`/`logs --previous`, node health and conditions, crictl, the resource metrics pipeline and metrics-server, `kubectl top`, cluster log architecture, known issues | 5, 7, 9, 11 | 4 |\n47-| 14 | Packing for the Voyage | *\"A chart is not a release, and templates are not the point\"* | IV | **D3.1** — from raw manifests to packages, Helm (chart structure, `Chart.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `templates/`, `charts/`, `crds/`, chart repositories, releases, upgrade and rollback), Kustomize (base and overlay), when each fits, CRDs shipped as chart content | 4, 6 | 5 |\n48-| 15 | The Chart Is the Truth | *\"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"* | IV | **D3.1** — the twelve-factor app, deployment strategies (rolling, Recreate, blue/green, canary, A/B), CI/CD vs GitOps, the four OpenGitOps principles, Argo CD (controller model, Git as source of truth, OutOfSync, sync, tracking branches/tags/commits, PreSync/Sync/PostSync hooks, drift detection, rollback), Flux, multi-cluster delivery | 3, 6, 14 | 7 |\n49-| 16 | Your Application, Their Cluster | *\"Four questions that separate 'my code is broken' from 'the platform is broken'\"* | IV | **D3.2** — application-scope triage flow, `kubectl debug` and ephemeral containers, init-container debugging, `exec`, `port-forward`, debugging Services and StatefulSets from the application side, local development and debugging, handoff boundary with Ch 13 | 5, 9, 13 | 4 |\n50-[Omitted long context line]\n51-[Omitted long context line]\n52-| 19 | Bearings Before Landfall | *\"Everything that connects, and the traps that don't\"* | VI | — (synthesis: cross-domain integration map, the top confusion pairs, exam-day pacing, The Lodestar walkthrough, what to do the week before) | 2–18 | 0 |\n53-| 20 | Full Mock Exam | *\"Ninety minutes. No notes. Find out.\"* | VI | — (assessment: full-length calibrated mock, weighted to 44/28/16/12, with worked answers and scoring rubric) | 2–18 | 0 |\n54-\n55-**Weight check:** 9+6+6+7+6+5+5 = **44** (D1) · 7+5+5+7+4 = **28** (D2) · 5+7+4 = **16** (D3) · 7+5 = **12** (D4) · total **100**.\n56-\n--\n89:Parts I and VI bracket the weighted material and carry no domain weight. Part I (\"Taking Departure\") does exam intelligence, the 2025-11-24 blueprint change, and the cloud-native framing that Chapter 17 later harvests. Part VI does synthesis and assessment.\n90-\n91-The Part titles carry the **Communications Officer** role family's atmospheric register — signals, dispatches, watches, standing off and making port — while the prose voice remains the single unified Lodestar voice. Differentiation is visual and atmospheric only, per the locked architectural rule.\n92-\n93-### Where are the prerequisite dependencies honored?\n94-\n95-Every edge B1 flagged as ordering-constraining is satisfied, and the Prerequisites column above is the machine-checkable record. The load-bearing ones:\n96-\n97-| B1 constraint | How the lineup honors it |\n98-|---|---|\n99-| Containerization precedes everything | Ch 2 is the first content chapter |\n100-| Objects/spec/status → controllers → Pod → workload resources | Ch 3 → 4 → 5 → 6, consecutive |\n101-| Scheduling needs Pod and Node, nothing from D2 | Ch 7, inside Part II |\n102-| Requests/limits precede scheduling and OOMKilled | Introduced Ch 5, applied Ch 7, diagnosed Ch 13 |\n103-| Service → Ingress → Gateway API, strictly | Ch 9 → Ch 10, in that sequence within Ch 10 |\n104-| NetworkPolicy taught once | Full treatment Ch 10; Ch 12 cross-bears in rather than duplicating |\n105-| Storage precedes StatefulSet's *full* treatment | Ch 6 introduces, Ch 11 completes, cross-beared both ways |\n106-| Troubleshooting and Debugging as one arc, two chapters | Ch 13 (platform scope) and Ch 16 (application scope), reciprocal cross-bearings |\n107-| Observability after microservices and service mesh | Ch 17 → Ch 18 |\n108-| GitOps needs the controller/reconciliation pattern | Ch 3 introduces, Ch 6 instantiates, Ch 15 generalizes |\n109-| Cloud-native *why* early, maturity/roster/governance late | Ch 1 plants the framing; Ch 17, second-to-last content chapter, delivers the institutional material |\n110-| ServiceAccount needed by RBAC subjects and Secret types | Planted in Ch 5 as Pod identity; full treatment Ch 12 |\n111-| CRDs/operators needed by Argo CD and Knative | Defined in Ch 6 as the controller pattern extended; full extensibility synthesis in Ch 17 |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e31eebcc-5cdf-41f6-9a4d-ad3448ff2836","uuid":"6c23d308-bf5d-43fe-9e59-4c4f6abfdbed","timestamp":"2026-08-24T03:42:19.036Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"19:| **I — Taking Departure** | What KCNA is, what changed, and how to read this book | 1 | — (orientation) | 0% |\n20-| **II — Ship, Cargo, and Company** | Containers, the cluster, the objects that describe it, and the components that run it | 2–8 | D1 Kubernetes Fundamentals | **44%** |\n21-| **III — Underway** | Keeping workloads reachabl